@*
    No data-theme here. Dark is the stylesheet's default and a reader whose system says light gets
    light — pinning the attribute meant a light-preferring reader was served dark regardless, which
    is not "dark is the default", it is "there is one theme".
*@
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="dark light" />
    <base href="/" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="app.css" />

    @*
        The SVG is the real icon and the .ico is the fallback for the browsers that still want one;
        anything that understands both takes the SVG, which is the one that stays sharp on a display
        the .ico has no size for.

        The mark is the asterisk out of the wordmark, drawn as strokes rather than typed — the site
        loads no font from anywhere, so a glyph here would be whatever the reader's browser fell
        back to. It carries its own plate and does not follow the reader's theme; favicon.svg says
        why, and the reason is the same 4.5:1 rule that makes the accent change value in app.css.
    *@
    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" sizes="32x32" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="apple-touch-icon.png" />
    @*
        The manifest is here for the name, the icon set and the colour a phone paints its chrome —
        and it declares display: browser, which means it does not ask to be installed. That is not a
        gap. These pages are static documents with no offline story; an installed shell that showed
        a blank frame on a train would be a small version of the thing this site exists not to do.
    *@
    <link rel="manifest" href="site.webmanifest" />

    @*
        Both, because neither theme is the real one. A single theme-color hands mobile Safari one of
        them as the truth and puts a dark bar above a light page for half the readers.
    *@
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#0f1113" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#f7f8f9" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />

    <HeadOutlet />
</head>
<body>
    <Routes />
    @*
        No blazor.web.js. Static server rendering only — every page here is a document, and the
        three places the design allows motion are plain CSS. Adding the script would opt the whole
        site into enhanced navigation and quietly break the no-script guarantee the facet panel
        and plain mode both depend on.
    *@
</body>
</html>
